    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d678">

                <pb facs="0725" n="711" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg711"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M) Chile/625</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to President Roosevelt </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-07">January 7,
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. President</hi>: I am enclosing for your
                    information a copy of a telegram sent the Department today.<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d678-f1"/> It is terribly disheartening at a time when we have
                    aligned Chile with us to the full in the vital matter of the Bolivian Junta, to
                    have these new plans to reduce everything, purchases and prices, to the serious
                    immediate disadvantage of Chile thrown in our laps. On the matter of wood pulp,
                    we all feel very strongly. I, personally, cannot yet understand why it is
                    impossible to give Chile 300 more tons of newsprint greatly needed, by taking
                    that merger amount from Argentina, our enemy, openly now our enemy, organizing
                    or trying to organize an anti-United States bloc in South America. It would
                    still leave Argentina, with five or six thousand tons a month as against 580
                    tons a month for this outstanding democratic country. The press is now attacking
                    on that score and this week it was the subject of discussion in the House of
                    Deputies. It is dangerous, and very hard to understand. This view is warmly
                    shared by every single member of my staff.</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Claude G. Bowers</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
